<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zamfara State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zamfara State is a state in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1996 from part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> State, with its capital at Gusau. It takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom of Zamfara, which flourished before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a> of the early 19th century. In 2000 it became the first Nigerian state to adopt Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> law in criminal matters, and in recent years it has suffered from banditry and kidnappings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zamfara-state.jpg" alt="Zamfara State" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himalayan Explorer based on work by Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigeria_Zamfara_State_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
